## Spec B1–B6 (docs/specs/syntax/async_lowering.spec.md Milestone 2A):
## an `async def` with no `.await` lowers to a single-state Future
## state machine. The first poll returns `Poll.Ready(42)`. Druxen by
## a hand-rolled poll loop using `Context.test_dummy` (B6) — no real
## executor needed.
##
## What this fixture pins:
##   B1 — `async def make_int() -> Int` typechecks; the resolved
##        return at the call site is a concrete state-machine class
##        (Milestone 2A's deviation from "some Future" — see the
##        spec's stop-condition note).
##   B2 — A class is synthesised per async fn (`__MakeIntFuture`).
##   B3 — The original `make_int()` returns the constructed class.
##   B5 — Calling poll once gets `Poll.Ready(42)` and the body runs
##        exactly once; subsequent polls would return Pending per
##        the spec's choice (not exercised here).
##   B6 — `Context.test_dummy` returns a Context value usable from
##        non-executor test code.

async def make_int() -> Int
  42
end

def main
  var fut = make_int()
  var ctx = Context.test_dummy
  match (&var fut).poll(&var ctx)
    Poll.Ready(v) -> puts "#{v}"
    Poll.Pending -> puts "pending"
  end
end
